{{!
    @template theme_boost/navbar

    NIT override of the Boost top navbar (a "طبق الأصل" of the legacy edumy
    header, rebuilt on Boost primitives — no edumy/cocoon dependency).

    Differences from theme_boost/navbar.mustache (re-diff on each Moodle upgrade;
    tracked in theme/nit/docs/override-budget.md):
      * Brand shows the compact logo AND the site name together (Boost shows one
        or the other).
      * The language menu sits on the left, right after the brand — and on a
        two-language site it is a single swap button, not a dropdown
        (output.navbar_language_menu).
      * A light/dark switch (output.navbar_mode_toggle) that selects a Brand
        Colors group rather than carrying a palette of its own.
      * The horizontal primary navigation is collapsed into a single gear-icon
        dropdown (built from `mobileprimarynav`), which carries a second,
        NIT-only group of management links (output.navbar_management_menu).
      * Right cluster order: site search (output.navbar_site_search), notifications
        (navbar_plugin_output), gear nav, user menu. Core's search_box is replaced by
        ours rather than shown alongside it — AC-4.22.1 asks for a single control.

    Visual treatment (navy field, gold wordmark) lives in
    theme/nit/scss/components/_navbar.scss, keyed off the `nit-navbar` class.

    Context variables required for this template:
    * output - The core renderer for the page.
    * sitename - The name of the site.
    * config - wwwroot / homeurl.
    * langmenu - Language menu context (from the primary output).
    * usermenu - User menu context (from the primary output).
    * mobileprimarynav - Array of primary nav items ({text, url, isactive}).
}}
<nav class="navbar fixed-top navbar-expand nit-navbar" aria-label="{{#str}}sitemenubar, admin{{/str}}"{{{ output.navbar_scroll_attributes }}}>
    <div class="container-fluid">
        <button class="navbar-toggler aabtn d-block d-md-none px-1 my-1 border-0" data-toggler="drawers" data-action="toggle" data-target="theme_boost-drawers-primary">
            <span class="navbar-toggler-icon"></span>
            <span class="visually-hidden">{{#str}}sidepanel, core{{/str}}</span>
        </button>

        {{! NIT: brand — compact logo AND the site name together. }}
        <a href="{{{ config.homeurl }}}" class="navbar-brand nit-navbar-brand d-none d-md-flex align-items-center m-0 me-3 p-0 aabtn">
            {{# output.should_display_navbar_logo }}
                {{! Core's own accessor, deliberately. theme_nit overrides
                    get_compact_logo_url() so it answers with the logo drawn for the
                    mode this page is in (Appearance → Logos), which means this line
                    — and every other place in Moodle that asks for the site logo —
                    needs no special knowledge of the light/dark switch. }}
                <img src="{{output.get_compact_logo_url}}" class="nit-navbar-logo me-2" alt="{{sitename}}">
            {{/ output.should_display_navbar_logo }}
            <span class="nit-navbar-title">{{{ sitename }}}</span>
        </a>

        {{! NIT: the site's own links (Home / Courses / About / Contact) as titles
            on the bar. Every row is read from core's "Custom menu items" setting
            (Appearance → Advanced theme settings), so an administrator adds,
            renames, reorders or deletes a link with no code and no release. }}
        {{{ output.navbar_custom_menu }}}

        <div id="usernavigation" class="navbar-nav ms-auto h-100 align-items-center">
            {{! NIT: AC-4.22.1 — ONE search control in the header, searching courses and
                categories together (theme_nit/navbar_site_search + local_nit_category).
                Core's own search box is deliberately not rendered beside it: two boxes
                that answer different questions is exactly what "a single search control"
                rules out. }}
            {{{ output.navbar_site_search }}}

            {{! NIT: language switcher — between the search control and the icons.
                Rendered via the renderer (output.navbar_language_menu) so it shows
                for logged-in users too: core folds the standalone menu into the
                user menu once logged in. }}
            <div class="nit-navbar-lang d-none d-md-flex align-items-center">
                {{{ output.navbar_language_menu }}}
            </div>

            {{! NIT: light/dark switch. It selects a Brand Colors group rather than
                carrying a palette of its own — an admin maps mode → group on the
                gallery "Change style" tab — so the click only swaps a class on
                <html> and the page re-skins with no reload. Empty (nothing drawn)
                when both modes point at the same group. }}
            {{{ output.navbar_mode_toggle }}}

            {{! NIT: the edit-mode switch was moved OUT of the bar and INTO the
                gear/navigation dropdown below (matches the legacy clean bar). }}

            {{! NIT: notifications / messaging (the bell) — kept as-is. }}
            {{# output.navbar_plugin_output }}
                {{{ . }}}
            {{/output.navbar_plugin_output}}

            {{! NIT: primary navigation collapsed into a single gear-icon dropdown
                (theme_nit/navbar_gear_menu): core's My courses / Site administration
                rows, the relocated edit-mode switch, and the management group.
                Empty — no gear on the bar at all — when all three would be, which
                is the case for a guest and for a student with nothing to open. }}
            {{{ output.navbar_gear_menu }}}

            {{! NIT: user menu (avatar) — Dashboard / Profile / Grades / Log out / Switch role. }}
            {{#usermenu}}
                <div class="d-flex align-items-stretch usermenu-container ms-1" data-region="usermenu">
                    {{> core/user_menu }}
                </div>
            {{/usermenu}}
        </div>
    </div>
</nav>

{{> theme_boost/primary-drawer-mobile }}
